        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            To seal redwood well, skip the clear coat and use a lightly pigmented, redwood-toned penetrating oil. Clean gently, brighten with oxalic acid, let the wood dry, apply thin coats and wipe off the excess. Seal new redwood as soon as it absorbs water instead of waiting a year, and keep steel away from the wood.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then maintain it on the water drop test: decks every one to three years, fences and gates a little less often, outdoor tables every season or two, and indoor pieces under a clear film only when they wear. Handle oily rags as the fire risk they are, and the red will last.
          </p>
